verdicts.note:
Nothing of Jowett's is lost:
- absolute -> 'beauty itself';
- separate -> 'existing by itself' ('and in itself' is a slight extra);
- simple -> 'one in form' ('always' is a slight extra);
- 'everlasting' is kept;
- 'without diminution and without increase, or any change' is kept;
- 'is imparted to' -> 'is shared by', the same relation of participation seen from the other side;
- 'ever-growing and perishing beauties of all other things' -> 'all the other beautiful things, which come into being and pass away'. This also removes round 2's misleading 'ever-growing', which a modern ear hears as 'constantly increasing'.
The two extras add no claim. The plain words work by ear at the climax. 'Without growing less' comes before 'shared', which blocks the misreading that the beauty is divided up. There are no contractions. One side effect is reported under paragraph_problems: 7.65 keeps Jowett's 'absolute beauty' twice and 'simple and divine', so the verbal link back to the climax is weaker than in round 2.
